1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:03,240
Hi,  and  welcome  back  to  Act.  Now

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:04,880
we  can  finally  start  thinking

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:06,879
about  solutions.  You  know  the

4
00:00:06,879 --> 00:00:08,679
problem  and  you  have  a  clearer

5
00:00:08,679 --> 00:00:11,000
idea  of  what  people  need.  The

6
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,280
next  step  is  not  to  choose  one

7
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:15,280
answer  immediately.  It  is  to

8
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:17,279
create  options.

9
00:00:17,279 --> 00:00:18,640
A  simple  way  to  start  is  with

10
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:21,279
the  question,  how  can  I?  For

11
00:00:21,279 --> 00:00:23,439
example,  instead  of  saying  I  will

12
00:00:23,439 --> 00:00:26,000
make  an  awareness  campaign,  ask,

13
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:27,599
how  can  I  help  people  get  the

14
00:00:27,599 --> 00:00:29,640
information  they  need?  That

15
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:31,160
question  gives  your  mind  more

16
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:33,040
room.  Maybe  the  answer  is  a

17
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:35,440
campaign.  Maybe  it  is  a  guide,  a

18
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:38,440
service,  a  meeting,  a  digital  tool

19
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:40,680
or  something  much  simpler.

20
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:43,399
First,  open  the  space.  Choosing

21
00:00:43,399 --> 00:00:44,541
comes  later.

22
00:00:44,541 --> 00:00:47,141
This  is  where  brainstorming  helps.

23
00:00:47,141 --> 00:00:48,781
Brainstorming  means  putting  down

24
00:00:48,781 --> 00:00:50,501
ideas  before  judging  them  too

25
00:00:50,501 --> 00:00:52,661
quickly.  For  a  few  minutes,  your

26
00:00:52,661 --> 00:00:54,140
job  is  simply  to  think  of  as

27
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:56,581
many  answers  as  you  can.  Write

28
00:00:56,581 --> 00:00:58,380
the  obvious  ideas,  write  the

29
00:00:58,380 --> 00:00:59,781
strange  ones,

30
00:00:59,781 --> 00:01:01,381
write  ideas  that  may  be  too

31
00:01:01,381 --> 00:01:03,701
expensive  or  too  difficult.

32
00:01:03,701 --> 00:01:05,660
You  are  not  promising  to  use  any

33
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:07,221
of  them.  You  are  just  giving

34
00:01:07,221 --> 00:01:09,461
yourself  choices.  If  you  reject

35
00:01:09,461 --> 00:01:11,820
every  idea  the  second  it  appears,

36
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:13,341
you  may  stop  before  you  reach

37
00:01:13,341 --> 00:01:14,660
something  better.

38
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:16,940
So,  keep  two  stages  separate.

39
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:19,981
First,  create  ideas.  After  that,

40
00:01:19,981 --> 00:01:21,661
check  which  ideas  are  realistic

41
00:01:21,661 --> 00:01:22,940
and  useful.

42
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:24,661
That  small  change  can  make  your

43
00:01:24,661 --> 00:01:26,582
thinking  much  more  creative.

44
00:01:26,582 --> 00:01:29,702
Let's  do  it.  Write  your  how  can  I

45
00:01:29,702 --> 00:01:31,822
question  at  the  top  of  a  page.

46
00:01:31,822 --> 00:01:33,702
Then  give  yourself  five  minutes

47
00:01:33,702 --> 00:01:35,221
and  write  as  many  possible

48
00:01:35,221 --> 00:01:36,982
solutions  as  you  can.

49
00:01:36,982 --> 00:01:39,422
One  idea  per  line  is  enough.

50
00:01:39,422 --> 00:01:41,221
Try  not  to  edit  yourself  while

51
00:01:41,221 --> 00:01:42,582
you  are  writing.

52
00:01:42,582 --> 00:01:44,102
When  the  five  minutes  are  over,

53
00:01:44,102 --> 00:01:46,062
look  at  everything  you  have.

54
00:01:46,062 --> 00:01:47,941
Combine  ideas  that  are  almost  the

55
00:01:47,941 --> 00:01:48,902
same.

56
00:01:48,902 --> 00:01:50,382
Then  choose  three  that  seems

57
00:01:50,382 --> 00:01:53,382
strongest.  Use  three  simple  checks.

58
00:01:53,382 --> 00:01:55,502
Can  I  realistically  do  this?  Does

59
00:01:55,502 --> 00:01:57,502
it  match  the  real  need need,  and

60
00:01:57,502 --> 00:01:59,862
could  it  bring  useful  benefit?

61
00:01:59,862 --> 00:02:01,902
Keep  those  three  ideas.  You  are

62
00:02:01,902 --> 00:02:03,742
not  choosing  the  final  one  yet.

63
00:02:03,742 --> 00:02:05,182
You  are  building  a  small  short

64
00:02:05,182 --> 00:02:06,582
list  that  you  can  compare  more

65
00:02:06,582 --> 00:02:07,873
carefully  next.

66
00:02:07,873 --> 00:02:10,752
Sometimes  one  good  idea  comes  from

67
00:02:10,752 --> 00:02:12,672
mixing  parts  of  several  smaller

68
00:02:12,672 --> 00:02:15,632
ideas.  Maybe  one  idea  is  training.

69
00:02:15,632 --> 00:02:17,833
Another  is  a  simple  online  tool

70
00:02:17,833 --> 00:02:19,672
and  another  is  improving  a  service

71
00:02:19,672 --> 00:02:22,233
that  already  exists.  You  may  find

72
00:02:22,233 --> 00:02:23,953
that  the  best  option  uses  a

73
00:02:23,953 --> 00:02:25,312
little  from  more  than  one  of

74
00:02:25,312 --> 00:02:26,312
them.

75
00:02:26,312 --> 00:02:27,793
That  is  why  it  helps  to  give

76
00:02:27,793 --> 00:02:29,633
ideas  some  space  before  cutting

77
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:30,753
them  down.

78
00:02:30,753 --> 00:02:32,353
The  first  idea  is  not  always  the

79
00:02:32,353 --> 00:02:34,193
best  one  and  the  most  exciting

80
00:02:34,193 --> 00:02:35,753
idea  is  not  always  the  most

81
00:02:35,753 --> 00:02:37,912
useful  one.  What  matters  is

82
00:02:37,912 --> 00:02:39,152
Finding  something  that  fits  the

83
00:02:39,152 --> 00:02:41,393
problem,  the  need,  and  what  you

84
00:02:41,393 --> 00:02:43,873
can  actually  manage.  Good  ideas

85
00:02:43,873 --> 00:02:45,513
become  stronger  when  you  compare

86
00:02:45,513 --> 00:02:47,553
them,  combine  useful  parts  and

87
00:02:47,553 --> 00:02:49,373
remove  what  does  not  help.

88
00:02:49,693 --> 00:02:51,332
There  is  another  nice  part  about

89
00:02:51,332 --> 00:02:53,453
community  projects.  While  you  are

90
00:02:53,453 --> 00:02:55,652
helping,  you  can  also  build  useful

91
00:02:55,652 --> 00:02:56,853
skills.

92
00:02:56,853 --> 00:02:58,173
Maybe  your  project  needs

93
00:02:58,173 --> 00:03:00,533
communication,  planning,  social

94
00:03:00,533 --> 00:03:03,653
media,  budgeting,  organizing  events,

95
00:03:03,653 --> 00:03:06,732
writing,  design,  or  technical  work.

96
00:03:06,732 --> 00:03:08,533
Doing  real  tasks  gives  you  a

97
00:03:08,533 --> 00:03:10,572
chance  to  practice  those  skills,

98
00:03:10,572 --> 00:03:12,253
not  just  read  about  them.

99
00:03:12,253 --> 00:03:14,013
Later,  that  experience  may  help

100
00:03:14,013 --> 00:03:15,453
you  explain  what  you  can  do  in  a

101
00:03:15,453 --> 00:03:18,492
CV,  portfolio,  interview,  or  future

102
00:03:18,492 --> 00:03:19,812
project.

103
00:03:19,812 --> 00:03:21,533
Volunteering  does  not  guarantee  a

104
00:03:21,533 --> 00:03:23,893
job,  of  course,  but  it  can  give

105
00:03:23,893 --> 00:03:25,493
you  real  situations  where  you

106
00:03:25,493 --> 00:03:28,493
learn,  make  mistakes,  improve  and

107
00:03:28,493 --> 00:03:30,252
understand  how  your  skills  work

108
00:03:30,252 --> 00:03:31,733
when  other  people  are  depending  on

109
00:03:31,733 --> 00:03:32,289
you.

110
00:03:32,289 --> 00:03:34,008
Imagine  you  want  to  work  in

111
00:03:34,008 --> 00:03:36,289
marketing  one  day.  You  understand

112
00:03:36,289 --> 00:03:38,088
social  media,  but  you  have  never

113
00:03:38,088 --> 00:03:40,369
planned  a  real  campaign.  A

114
00:03:40,369 --> 00:03:42,049
volunteer  project  could  give  you  a

115
00:03:42,049 --> 00:03:44,169
small  place  to  practice.  Set  a

116
00:03:44,169 --> 00:03:46,888
goal,  create  posts,  organize  the

117
00:03:46,888 --> 00:03:49,169
work,  watch  the  response,  and

118
00:03:49,169 --> 00:03:50,648
learn  what  worked.

119
00:03:50,648 --> 00:03:52,369
Or  maybe  you  want  experience  with

120
00:03:52,369 --> 00:03:53,529
finance.

121
00:03:53,529 --> 00:03:55,369
Helping  a  community  organization

122
00:03:55,369 --> 00:03:57,568
with  simple  financial  tasks  with

123
00:03:57,568 --> 00:03:59,249
the  right  guidance  can  give  you

124
00:03:59,249 --> 00:04:01,129
useful  practice.

125
00:04:01,129 --> 00:04:03,048
The  point  is  not  to work  for  free

126
00:04:03,048 --> 00:04:04,249
forever.

127
00:04:04,249 --> 00:04:05,649
The  point  is  to  notice  that

128
00:04:05,649 --> 00:04:07,649
service  can  also  become  a  place

129
00:04:07,649 --> 00:04:08,929
to  grow.

130
00:04:08,929 --> 00:04:10,129
You  can  help  with  something

131
00:04:10,129 --> 00:04:12,249
meaningful  while  building  experience

132
00:04:12,249 --> 00:04:13,489
that  may  be  useful  in  your

133
00:04:13,489 --> 00:04:14,289
future.

134
00:04:14,289 --> 00:04:16,409
Now  look  at  your  three  solution

135
00:04:16,409 --> 00:04:17,609
ideas.

136
00:04:17,609 --> 00:04:20,168
What  skills  would  each  one  need?

137
00:04:20,168 --> 00:04:22,848
Be  honest.  Maybe  one  idea  needs  a

138
00:04:22,848 --> 00:04:25,129
skill  you  already  have.  Maybe

139
00:04:25,129 --> 00:04:26,569
another  needs  something  you  have

140
00:04:26,569 --> 00:04:28,329
never  done  before.

141
00:04:28,329 --> 00:04:29,649
That  does  not  mean  you  have  to

142
00:04:29,649 --> 00:04:31,609
throw  the  idea  away.

143
00:04:31,609 --> 00:04:33,249
It  may  mean  you  need  to  learn

144
00:04:33,249 --> 00:04:35,648
first.  Ask  someone  experienced  for

145
00:04:35,648 --> 00:04:38,209
advice.  Choose  a  simpler  version

146
00:04:38,209 --> 00:04:40,369
or  pick  a  different  option.

147
00:04:40,369 --> 00:04:41,968
Knowing  your  limits  is  part  of

148
00:04:41,968 --> 00:04:43,849
good  leadership  too.

149
00:04:43,849 --> 00:04:45,689
Confidence  is  useful,  but

150
00:04:45,689 --> 00:04:47,329
pretending  you  can  do  something

151
00:04:47,329 --> 00:04:48,969
when  you  cannot  can  hurt  the

152
00:04:48,969 --> 00:04:51,528
project  and  the  people  involved.

153
00:04:51,528 --> 00:04:53,929
It  is  completely  fine  to  say,  I

154
00:04:53,929 --> 00:04:55,729
need  to  learn  this  before  I  take

155
00:04:55,729 --> 00:04:57,205
responsibility  for  it.

156
00:04:57,205 --> 00:04:59,044
Take  a  minute  and  write  down  at

157
00:04:59,044 --> 00:05:00,644
least  three  skills  you  would  like

158
00:05:00,644 --> 00:05:02,165
to  improve  while  working  on  your

159
00:05:02,165 --> 00:05:03,685
project.  They  could  be

160
00:05:03,685 --> 00:05:06,565
communication,  listening,  planning,

161
00:05:06,565 --> 00:05:08,924
decision  making,  problem  solving,

162
00:05:08,924 --> 00:05:11,844
leadership,  budgeting,  negotiation,

163
00:05:11,844 --> 00:05:13,685
or  a  practical  skill  connected  to

164
00:05:13,685 --> 00:05:15,805
your  idea.  Choose  skills  that  you

165
00:05:15,805 --> 00:05:17,965
will  actually  use.  Do  not  pick

166
00:05:17,965 --> 00:05:19,644
something  only  because  it  sounds

167
00:05:19,644 --> 00:05:20,925
impressive.

168
00:05:20,925 --> 00:05:22,644
Then  look  at  your  three  solution

169
00:05:22,644 --> 00:05:24,884
ideas  again  and  ask  which  one

170
00:05:24,884 --> 00:05:26,285
gives  you  a  real  chance  to

171
00:05:26,285 --> 00:05:28,244
practice  those  skills. while  still

172
00:05:28,244 --> 00:05:30,365
helping  the  people  you  care  about.

173
00:05:30,365 --> 00:05:31,685
Your  project  should  serve  the

174
00:05:31,685 --> 00:05:33,885
community  first,  but  it  can  also

175
00:05:33,885 --> 00:05:35,284
become  a  useful  place  for  your

176
00:05:35,284 --> 00:05:36,038
own  growth.

177
00:05:36,038 --> 00:05:38,358
Before  moving  on,  check  the  three

178
00:05:38,358 --> 00:05:40,317
ideas  against  the  need  you  wrote

179
00:05:40,317 --> 00:05:43,158
earlier.  For  each  one,  ask,  how

180
00:05:43,158 --> 00:05:45,318
exactly  does  this  help?

181
00:05:45,318 --> 00:05:46,478
If  you  cannot  explain  the

182
00:05:46,478 --> 00:05:48,438
connection  in  one  or  two  simple

183
00:05:48,438 --> 00:05:51,318
sentences,  the  idea  may  not  fit.

184
00:05:51,318 --> 00:05:53,477
Change  it  or  remove  it.

185
00:05:53,477 --> 00:05:55,198
Also  check  that  your  three  ideas

186
00:05:55,198 --> 00:05:57,157
are  really  different.  Three

187
00:05:57,157 --> 00:05:59,198
versions  of  the  same  idea  do  not

188
00:05:59,198 --> 00:06:00,798
give  you  much  choice.

189
00:06:00,798 --> 00:06:02,518
Go  back  and  create  another  option

190
00:06:02,518 --> 00:06:03,838
if  you  need  to.

191
00:06:03,838 --> 00:06:05,878
This  part  is  worth  the  effort.

192
00:06:05,878 --> 00:06:07,358
You  are  trying  to  avoid  choosing

193
00:06:07,358 --> 00:06:09,838
an  idea  just  because  you  like  it.

194
00:06:09,838 --> 00:06:11,198
The  final  solution  should  make

195
00:06:11,198 --> 00:06:13,318
sense  for  the  problem,  the  people,

196
00:06:13,318 --> 00:06:14,678
and  what  can  realistically  be

197
00:06:14,678 --> 00:06:15,329
done.

198
00:06:15,329 --> 00:06:18,488
So,  now  you  have  two  useful  things

199
00:06:18,488 --> 00:06:20,929
to  carry  forward.  Three  possible

200
00:06:20,929 --> 00:06:23,128
solutions  and  a  short  list  of

201
00:06:23,128 --> 00:06:25,249
skills  you  want  to  build.

202
00:06:25,249 --> 00:06:28,128
You  did  not  rush  into  one  answer.

203
00:06:28,128 --> 00:06:30,449
You  gave  yourself  choices,  checked

204
00:06:30,449 --> 00:06:32,528
them  against  the  real  need  and

205
00:06:32,528 --> 00:06:35,129
kept  the  strongest  options.

206
00:06:35,129 --> 00:06:36,688
That  gives  you  a  much  better

207
00:06:36,688 --> 00:06:39,009
place  to  make  a  decision  from.

208
00:06:39,009 --> 00:06:41,409
Next,  we  will  compare  those  ideas

209
00:06:41,409 --> 00:06:43,528
more  carefully  and  choose  the  one

210
00:06:43,528 --> 00:06:45,329
that  fits  best.

211
00:06:45,329 --> 00:06:47,649
The  goal  is  not  to  find  a  perfect

212
00:06:47,649 --> 00:06:49,809
solution.  It  is  to  find  one  that

213
00:06:49,809 --> 00:06:52,689
is  useful,  realistic,  connected  to

214
00:06:52,689 --> 00:06:54,969
the  real  need  and  possible  to

215
00:06:54,969 --> 00:06:57,449
improve  as  you  learn  more.

216
00:06:57,449 --> 00:06:59,169
Keep  your  notes  with  you  because

217
00:06:59,169 --> 00:07:00,568
we  will  use  them  again  in  the

218
00:07:00,568 --> 00:07:02,089
next  step.

219
00:07:02,089 --> 00:07:03,929
That's  it  for  today.  Keep  your

220
00:07:03,929 --> 00:07:05,769
three  ideas  and  your  skill  list

221
00:07:05,769 --> 00:07:07,208
with  you  and  I'll  see  you  in  the

222
00:07:07,208 --> 00:07:08,245
next  lesson.

